call_details.response:
The board is oriented with row 0 at the top. Gravity is already satisfied in every column, and the legal columns (those whose top cell is empty) are exactly 0, 1, 2, 5 and 6.

A exhaustive static scan of every empty cell shows that the single location at which an x would complete four consecutive pieces is (1,1) (the diagonal (1,1)-(2,2)-(3,3)-(4,4)). That cell is not presently reachable: the lowest empty cell of column 1 is (2,1). Consequently the only move that turns (1,1) into an immediate winning threat is the drop into column 1, which fills (2,1) and leaves (1,1) as the new lowest empty cell of that column.

No other legal drop creates a winning threat (immediate or unlocked). In particular:

- column 0 produces only a blocked three-in-a-row on the diagonal (5,0)-(4,1)-(3,2);
- column 2 produces only pairs;
- column 5 produces only a vertical pair;
- column 6 produces only a blocked three-in-a-row on the diagonal (5,6)-(4,5)-(3,4).

Thus column 1 is the unique forcing move. After the forced block by o, the side to move still retains the initiative and the newly placed x at (2,1) participates in future connecting patterns. All alternative first moves surrender that initiative without creating any compensating threat.

Final Answer: 1

